DTrace integration on dev, 2026-10-13

Scope and isolation
Saved probe patches from rounds 4 through 20 and the additional round 21
were reconstructed and integrated on dev at c222f34d39557e8bb07f4971bc63838d37816415.
The original /usr/src checkout on fix/kldload-broker-guidance retains its
unrelated pending work. No live installation, module loading, policy changes,
publication, push, or reboot was performed.

The integration preserves dev's FIDL migration and libservice.so.2.
BSDSysctl admission failures cover FIDL connection creation and binding.
liboes retains FIDL generation and adds its DTrace build integration.
ObsoleteFiles.inc and UPDATING retain both the migration and fixed-name BSD
PTY retirement. Modern pts/PTMX remains supported. Profile whitespace was
normalized; historical per-round source hashes describe their saved snapshots.

Latest two passes
50 new contracts: 45 userland and 5 kernel, plus 9 analysis profiles.
Six libraries: libservice, libchannel, libcapsulert, liboes, libtimecmp,
libshmring. Executables: Capsule, Switchboard, BSDDevice, BSDExtension,
BSDMemory, BSDSysctl, Zsh, mac_abacd, mac_abac_ctl, oeslogger.
MAC ABAC records bypass, rule and default decisions with operation, rule/set,
result and missing-label indicators. OpenES records accepted authorization
masks and rejected responses. Library/tool probes expose scalar error/status,
validation, allocation/admission, timeout and lifecycle context. No credentials,
command strings or event payloads are added. Kernel additions are guarded
against cold boot and do not instrument tracing internals.
See COVERAGE-TWENTIETH and COVERAGE-TWENTYFIRST for contracts and limitations.

Validation
All 42 native dev integration builds passed with DTrace both enabled and
disabled (32 target builds plus 10 dependency builds). All 45 new userland
contracts have linked sites and expected arity; providers are absent when
disabled. Both changed kernel modules link, four source on/off compilations
pass, and all five new kernel contracts have linked sites (six total sites).
All selected modified kernel C/header files match the validated /usr/src
sources byte for byte.

The preceding per-round tests cover service runtime, claim parsing, liboes
ownership/invalid-descriptor/allocation failures, and upstream Zsh execution,
grammar and assignment tests. Sixteen userland no-hooks .text comparisons
are identical. After normalizing diagnostic file/line constants, OpenES kernel
.text is identical; ABAC has compiler control-flow layout differences at equal
text size, so byte identity is NOT claimed. Its disabled object has no cold or
probe symbol references from the new instrumentation. These comparisons do
not establish relocation equivalence or runtime behavior.

The complete 451-profile catalog was rendered in plain and PID/duration forms:
820 offline compilations passed (410 profiles); 82 checks (41 unchanged
profiles) require unavailable live pid-provider or typed kernel probe metadata.
All changed/new profiles compile. No /dev/dtrace was available, so actual
probe firing, privileged authorization workflows and the new kernel were not
exercised on a running system. No full world was built for this integration.

Artifacts and reproduction
/usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-commit-validation contains build/results.json,
extra-build/results.json, integrated-dof.json, profile-results.json, census/,
the exact reconstructed patches and validation driver scripts. Per-round
artifacts remain under /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-<round>-validation. Drivers use
the standard /usr/obj object root and all available logical CPUs.
The saved integration-check driver performs the initial offline catalog pass;
the followup supplies required profile parameters and classifies live-provider
limitations. Original kernel code-generation comparisons with diagnostic line
changes are retained; normalized-codegen-results.json records the followup.

TRACING-DESIGN-REVIEW records the Fuchsia, CADETS and HyperTrace review.
It is a proposal, not a tracing-engine import or performance claim.
